A||||1||Philz||Erratic High Idle||||1||
Z||000000||Philz||12-11-2001||09:34 PM||||My 1987 2.5 liter 5 spd. On certain occasions it will start and hold a idle of 2900 to 3000 rpms. This will only happen after the engine is hot. I have tried shutting the engine off and restarting, once this worked but more often not.  It will go for days with no problems then the above mentioned problem will occur. I have checked the ecm memory for problems it states none. Even when it appears to have no problem with the idle it takes approx. 15 to 20 seconds for the engine to come down from high idle (1500 to 1800 rpm) to 1000rpm.  That is as low as it will idle. I have replaced the TBI and the throttle plate sensor.  Could the ECM be bad? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||12-11-2001||10:06 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Philz:<BR><B>My 1987 2.5 liter 5 spd. On certain occasions it will start and hold a idle of 2900 to 3000 rpms. This will only happen after the engine is hot. I have tried shutting the engine off and restarting, once this worked but more often not.  It will go for days with no problems then the above mentioned problem will occur. I have checked the ecm memory for problems it states none. Even when it appears to have no problem with the idle it takes approx. 15 to 20 seconds for the engine to come down from high idle (1500 to 1800 rpm) to 1000rpm.  That is as low as it will idle. I have replaced the TBI and the throttle plate sensor.  Could the ECM be bad? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Let me welcome you to the Forum!<P>Actually, you are not far off on that with the ECM being bad.  There was a TSB (technical service bulliten) that GM put out about this issue.  Its not neccessarily a recall, but more of a set of directions on how to solve a prevalent problem once the car was at the dealers to solve it.  Abnormally high hot idle is one of the issues.  There are new PROMs (computer chips) available to rememdy this situation, but I do also believe that the fuel pump has to be modified for your case.  My 87 2.5 does a similar thing, it will idle super high when I first start it every once and a while, and will take forever to come down to a normal 900 when its warmed up.  Definatly a computer issue.  I am swapping in a V6 soon so I am not going to bother trying to fix it.  I will try to search for the topic that explains the PROM TSB in more detail...<P>EDIT:  try this link <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20000503-2-000085.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20000503-2-000085.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by FieroJoe (edited 12-11-2001).]||||reg||1
